   "properties": {
    "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
   },
   "title": "How to use · v1.0.0",
   "widgets_values": [
    "## How to use · SeedVR2 Upscaler (picture) v1.0.0\n\n**What it does:** makes a picture bigger and restores detail in one step with SeedVR2 7B (int8). No prompt. Core ComfyUI nodes only.\n\n1. **Your inputs**: load your picture in **Picture to upscale** (it opens with ComfyUI's own `example.png`). **Upscale factor** 2 doubles width and height. Keep the result (your size x factor) at about 8 megapixels or less on a 12 GB card (see below).\n2. **Models**: download the two files from the note below. Nothing to change here.\n3. **Enlarge + encode**: the picture is enlarged with lanczos first; SeedVR2 then works at the final size. Transparency is kept.\n4. **Restore**: 1 step, CFG 1, euler / simple, from the template. The **Seed** sits here: another number gives other fine detail (leaves, threads), the same picture otherwise.\n5. **Decode + colour match**: `lab` pulls the colours back to the original. The template uses `none`; `none` was not run here.\n6. **Save**: PNG in `ComfyUI/output/SeedVR2/`. **Before / after** shows the original and the result with a slider when the Nodes 2.0 view is on; the classic view shows \"Node 2.0 only\" there.\n\n**What to expect** (RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell SFF Edition 24 GB, ComfyUI 0.45.3)\n- 1776x1184 photo, factor 2 = 3552x2368 (8.4 MP): 12 to 14 s. VRAM 10.0 to 11.1 GiB with no limit, 9.7 GiB in a 12 GiB budget, 9.5 GiB in a 10 GiB budget, same picture to the pixel.\n- Factor 3 on the same photo (5328x3552, 18.9 MP): 28 s, but VRAM went to 13.5 GiB in the 12 GiB budget and 11.9 GiB in the 10 GiB budget: the budget did not hold, so this size is not for a 12 GB card.\n- `example.png`, factor 2 = 1536x1536: 3 s.\n- VRAM = peak of the ComfyUI process in nvidia-smi, in GiB (MiB / 1024; a 12 GB card has 12.0). Budget runs: a second ComfyUI started with `--reserve-vram` so that it may use only 12 or 10 GiB. Not verified on a real 12 GB card.\n\n**Good to know**\n- SeedVR2 redraws fine texture (skin, leaves, text). Compare before you keep it. A sharp picture gains little.\n"
   ]
  },
